					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tidWYsA977fUMknc6",
					"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
					"reference": "B52",
					"local_notes": "Top sprinting speed x2 (Move 14 sprints at 28 yd/s, about 90 km/h). Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: Custodians move with a speed that stuns even Space Marines who fight beside them; Chris Wraight's Custodes novels describe them crossing distances in moments that should take a squad minutes.\n\nDesign: Enhanced Move (Ground) 1 (B52, 20 points) at the user's direction; a full level against the Astartes' half level keeps Custodes clearly the faster of the two.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"points_per_level": 20,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "trMGO0Yxo3aYwZl5D",
					"name": "Unfazeable",
					"reference": "B95",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian has stood in the Sanctum Imperialis for ten thousand years, fought Horus's own Sons of Horus, boarded daemon-infested hulks, and duelled things with no right to exist. Black Library and Codex background never show a Custodian rattled, hesitant, or surprised by anything short of the direct will of the Emperor.\n\nDesign: Unfazeable (B95, 15 points): exempt from Fright Checks, and reaction modifiers do not affect the Custodian (it is the Basic Set trait that grants outright fear immunity, which Fearlessness, being a leveled bonus to Fright Checks, does not). This matches the framework's flat \"immune to fear\" instruction rather than a leveled resistance.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "ttdQjNMb2k-mOG8G9",
					"name": "Unaging",
					"reference": "B95",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The Ten Thousand have served since before the Imperium existed; named Custodians such as Valerian, Aleya, and the long-lived characters of Wraight's novels are treated as ancient by any human standard without any hint of physical decline. The exact biological mechanism (whether it is a one-off transformation or an ongoing process) is not detailed in the fiction consulted for this build.\n\nDesign: Unaging (B95, 15 points): no HT penalties or death checks from old age, matching \"lives for millennia\" without claiming a specific immortality mechanism the sources don't describe.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tqS9NU2kx6JevAj3r",
					"name": "Combat Reflexes",
					"reference": "B43",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Centuries of drilling against the finest opponents the Imperium can offer (including each other) leave a Custodian essentially unshakeable in combat, never fumbling a reaction to a sudden threat.\n\nDesign: Combat Reflexes (B43, 15 points), as the Astartes package.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tOx_1Kfg3jTmlJQmM",
					"name": "Custodian Senses",
					"reference": "B16",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Codex background credits Custodians with reflexes and situational awareness that let them track and parry attacks other elites simply cannot perceive in time; this is modelled as raw Perception rather than the itemised acute-sense organs the Astartes package uses, since no source breaks a Custodian's senses down organ by organ.\n\nDesign: Increased Perception +4 (B16, 5/level, 20 points): a broad \"notices everything\" bonus, deliberately not split into Astartes- style Acute Vision/Hearing/Smell packages since the fiction doesn't describe Custodian senses that granularly. The +4 is a real attribute bonus (Per 20 on a Guardian's Per 16 base), not a note.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 4,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "per",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "TmbD-kK_agqc0Gw3a",
					"name": "Custodian Regeneration",
					"local_notes": "Lore: No source gives a canonical recovery figure for a Custodian; the working assumption here is that a gene-wrought body heals faster than an Astartes', not that it is invulnerable. This project reads that as healing markedly faster than the Astartes package's Larraman's-Organ-based Rapid Healing. The Regeneration (Slow) piece is small and rarely matters in play; a GM may drop it and refund 10.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Slow, B80, 10 points) for passive healing even without rest, plus Very Rapid Healing (B80, 15 points, quadruple effective HT for natural recovery) for a step above the Astartes' plain Rapid Healing (double effective HT). 25 points total.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tRqBpXBYfZ1od-KRn",
							"name": "Regeneration (Slow)",
							"reference": "B80",
							"local_notes": "Recovers 1 HP per 12 hours even without rest or first aid.",
							"base_points": 10
						},
						{
							"id": "tR3DtsCqDzUU82Z8w",
							"name": "Very Rapid Healing",
							"reference": "B80",
							"base_points": 15
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "TLZQ3nFnL2izVImqr",
					"name": "Custodian Constitution",
					"local_notes": "Lore: No single source itemises separate anti-toxin organs for Custodians the way Codex Astartes background does for the Preomnor and Oolitic Kidney; what the fiction consistently shows is a Custodian simply shrugging off poisons, plagues, and pain that would down anyone else outright.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Very Common: Metabolic Hazards (disease, poison, toxins, radiation sickness and the like), Immunity tier - base 30, x1 for Immunity = 30 points, deliberately a single unified trait rather than the Astartes package's several organ-specific ones (Preomnor 10 + Oolitic Kidney 15 + part of Melanchromic Organ 11 = 36 split across three separate hazards): a Custodian's constitution doesn't have gaps between what different organs happen to cover. High Pain Threshold (B59, 10 points) as the Astartes package. Less Sleep 4 (B65, 8 points) and Reduced Consumption 3 (B80, 6 points) model the framework's \"needs almost no sleep or food\". 54 points total.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tslCAFucNOAXnKja7",
							"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
							"reference": "B81",
							"local_notes": "Resistant (Very Common: Disease, Poison, Toxins, etc.), Immunity.",
							"base_points": 30
						},
						{
							"id": "t8KSC9o7ojp7rQQ6_",
							"name": "High Pain Threshold",
							"reference": "B59",
							"base_points": 10
						},
						{
							"id": "tdO0jo73jrSYzjkZ5",
							"name": "Less Sleep",
							"reference": "B65",
							"local_notes": "Needs 4 hours less sleep per night (max level); a Custodian's vigil is near-constant.",
							"points_per_level": 2,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 4
						},
						{
							"id": "tYy_MeiiGze6FyxKP",
							"name": "Reduced Consumption",
							"reference": "B80",
							"local_notes": "Needs an eighth of normal food and water (level 3).",
							"points_per_level": 2,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 3
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TjosoCiyEEs20UEMz",
			"name": "Custodian Oath and Mindset",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Custodians are famously remote even from other Imperial elites - Inquisitors, Chapter Masters and High Lords alike are received with cool, absolute courtesy rather than camaraderie - and Wraight's novels repeatedly frame this as focus rather than arrogance: nothing matters to a Custodian except the duty in front of him. The historic vow keeping the Legio on Terra is well documented in current Codex background and its lifting during the Indomitus Crusade is likewise current-edition canon, not a house rule.\n\nDesign: Duty (-15) and Sense of Duty (-5) mirror the Astartes package's structure but narrow the in-group to the Legio Custodes itself, per the \"aloof even from fellow elites\" note. Code of Honor (-10) is written from scratch rather than reusing Codex Astartes' text, since a Custodian answers to no Chapter. The Vow is included but disabled by default for the reason given above; enabling it is a straight -10 swap, nothing else in this file needs to change. Aloof (-1 Quirk) is the cheapest possible flag for the \"doesn't warm to outsiders\" personality note repeated across the fiction, kept small because nothing in the sources supports a harsher social disadvantage (Custodians aren't shown as cruel or friendless, just focused). Net: -15-5-10-1 = -31 with the Vow disabled, -41 enabled.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tBY9CzWSwgR8LeIzC",
					"name": "Duty (The Emperor and the Throneworld)",
					"reference": "B133",
					"local_notes": "Almost all the time, Extremely Hazardous.",
					"base_points": -15
				},
				{
					"id": "ttECsUEeeptixRAyp",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (The Ten Thousand)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Small group: fellow Custodians and the Throne they guard, not the wider Imperium.",
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "tA_JHdpB7_PN8vqHm",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Custodian's Creed)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Never leave a post while the Emperor lives; never speak falsely to the Throne; accept correction from no authority outside the Legio Custodes and the Emperor himself.",
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tzgi4CrUtCzGiW3qE",
					"name": "Vow (Never Leave the Emperor's Side / Terra)",
					"reference": "B161",
					"local_notes": "Major Vow. Disabled by default: this project follows current Codex background and the Indomitus Crusade-era novels, where Guilliman as Lord Commander formally released Custodians for wider deployment after ten thousand years of confinement to Terra. Enable this for a Custodian bound by the older, absolute reading of the vow (pre-Indomitus, or a GM who wants the classic \"the Ten Thousand never leave Terra\" status quo).",
					"base_points": -10,
					"disabled": true
				},
				{
					"id": "tMAwLLiZRez-N0-yq",
					"name": "Aloof",
					"local_notes": "Rarely engages with anyone outside the Legio Custodes as an equal, the Emperor excepted.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tDY_tUbkaVA2TURVa",
			"name": "Rank (Legio Custodes)",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Every Custodian outranks nearly the entire Imperial military and ecclesiastical hierarchy by simple virtue of standing at the Emperor's side; Codex background and Wraight's novels both show even Inquisitors and Chapter Masters deferring to a Custodian's word on matters of the Throneworld's security.\n\nDesign: Rank (B29), 5 points/level, 6 levels = 30 points: double the Battle-Brother template's Rank 3 (15 points), reflecting that even a rank-and-file Custodian Guard carries more institutional weight than a veteran Astartes sergeant.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "t_9fp_KhsdGXb-iAq",
			"name": "Reputation (The Emperor's Companions)",
			"reference": "B27",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian's golden plate is recognized on sight across the Imperium as the Emperor's own guard - awe, terror or reverent relief depending on who's looking - a stronger and more universal version of the reaction an Astartes' Chapter heraldry gets.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B27), priced informally above the Battle-Brother template's own Reputation (Angel of Death, 5 points for a +2 reaction) at 10 points for a +3 reaction recognized by essentially everyone in the Imperium, not just soldiery - the same design shortcut the Astartes file uses rather than a full frequency/scope calculation, since GURPS Reputation pricing is explicitly meant to be eyeballed against comparable published examples.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"base_points": 10,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from anyone in the Imperium who recognizes Custodian gold, at any range they can be seen",
					"amount": 3
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "taJnwdNSJCIDXJzzZ",
			"name": "Personal Advancement",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Even among the Ten Thousand, centuries or millennia of live combat against the Imperium's worst threats leave individual Custodians measurably sharper than the day they were raised to full service; Wraight's novels distinguish named, veteran Custodians from newly-elevated ones in exactly these terms.\n\nDesign: DX+1 (20) + IQ+1 (20) + HT+1 (10) + Will+1 (5) + Per+2 (10) = 65 points, priced at the real attribute costs with nothing added. The DX+1 is the training bump that takes the Guardian to the user-set DX 20; IQ, HT, Will and Per are a veteran's seasoning on top of the physiology package (which already meets the framework's IQ 13, HT 16, Will 16 targets).",
			"tags": [
				"Physical",
				"Mental"
			],
			"base_points": 65,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "dx",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "iq",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "ht",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "will",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "per",
					"amount": 2
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tvn25LaCHEar3n6_I",
			"name": "Weapon Master (Custodes Power Weapons)",
			"reference": "B99",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Custodian Guard train on nothing but the Legio's own master-crafted arms for centuries before ever taking the field; Wraight's novels and current Codex background both treat that training as qualitatively different from even an Astartes veteran's, not merely longer.\n\nDesign: Weapon Master (B99), small class of weapons (guardian spear, sentinel blade, castellan axe, misericordia, pyrithite/adrasite spear, interceptor lance - the Custodes power-weapon family), 30 points: adds extra damage with those weapons and removes the reduced-ST-below-minimum penalty, matching centuries of dedicated drilling with the Legio's own signature arms rather than general competence with any blade.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 30
		},
		{
			"id": "t4UrJxg5o8iI4ylKh",
			"name": "Ambidexterity",
			"reference": "B39",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Custodian Guard are frequently shown fighting two-handed (a blade and a shield, or paired sentinel blades) with no favoured hand, a small but recurring visual detail in Codex art.\n\nDesign: Ambidexterity (B39, 5 points): no off-hand penalty for two-weapon or shield-and-blade fighting.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 5
		},
		{
			"id": "tgpZQgoFY5dxQ661q",
			"name": "Trained by a Master",
			"reference": "B93",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian's training is described as without peer in the Imperium - centuries under instructors who are themselves the Legio's most experienced warriors, a step beyond even an Astartes Chapter's own training cadre.\n\nDesign: Trained by a Master (B93, 30 points): the GURPS advantage for training so complete it grants bonus defenses and combat options no amount of ordinary skill points buys, reserved in Basic Set for exactly this kind of \"best in the setting\" martial pedigree.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 30
		}
	],
